{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0dfb026aaad6c9340169bb16e10690819184ae46a281e56075", "created": 1773868769, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "A **PATHOLOGIST** is a medical doctor and key member of the multidisciplinary care team who **diagnoses and characterizes disease—especially cancer—by examining cells, tissues, organs, and body-fluid samples** collected during biopsy, surgery, or other procedures. The PATHOLOGIST typically **receives biopsy cores, surgical specimens, lymph nodes, bone marrow, urine cytology, and other samples**, prepares and stains them, and **examines them under a microscope** (including cytology and related laboratory techniques) to identify abnormal, precancerous, or cancerous cells.\n\nIn cancer care, the PATHOLOGIST **confirms whether cancer is present**, determines the **tumor type and subtype (tumor histology)**, and describes key microscopic features used for diagnosis and management (e.g., cell size, shape, and overall appearance). The PATHOLOGIST often provides **tumor grading** by comparing cancer cells with normal cells and assigning standardized grade components (such as **Gleason patterns**, contributing to the Gleason score and Grade Group in prostate cancer). The PATHOLOGIST also evaluates **extent of disease**, including **depth of invasion**, **tumor invasion into surrounding tissues**, and whether **lymph nodes** contain cancer.\n\nThe PATHOLOGIST assesses critical surgical findings such as whether **cancer cells are present at surgical margins**, which influences staging and treatment planning. Using tissue and lymph node findings, the PATHOLOGIST may assign a **surgical/pathologic stage** (e.g., stage I–III) and identify high-risk or special diagnostic features (such as benign findings, invasion, or entities like NIFTP in thyroid pathology) that guide decisions about additional surgery (e.g., completion thyroidectomy) or further therapy.\n\nThe PATHOLOGIST also performs and interprets **biomarker testing**, including **immunohistochemistry (IHC)**, such as measuring **estrogen and/or progesterone receptor** expression and reporting receptor quantity within tumor cells. In hematologic malignancies, the PATHOLOGIST (including hematopathology expertise) reviews **bone marrow** to diagnose conditions such as **acute myeloid leukemia (AML)**. In lymphoma evaluation, the PATHOLOGIST helps establish and characterize the diagnosis, noting that prior treatments (e.g., steroids) can sometimes alter cell appearance and complicate interpretation. The PATHOLOGIST may also **re-review prior tumor tissue** when needed to confirm or refine a diagnosis (e.g., ovarian cancer).\n\nFindings from the PATHOLOGIST are documented in a **pathology report**, which summarizes diagnosis, grade, stage-related features, margins, lymph node status, and relevant biomarkers, and directly informs clinical recommendations and treatment decisions.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 562, "prompt_tokens": 1196, "total_tokens": 1758, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}